 ## Overview
-Part-based MHE-augmented NeRF is a method for learning neural volumetric videos of dynamic humans from sparse view videos in minutes with competitive visual quality [S1]. It addresses the challenge of quickly reconstructing free-viewpoint videos of dynamic humans by decomposing the canonical human body into multiple anatomical parts, assigning each part its own Multiresolution Hash Encoding (MHE)-augmented NeRF network [S1]. This approach efficiently distributes representational power to handle varying shape and texture complexity across different body parts (e.g., face vs. torso) and accelerates training convergence compared to a single unified NeRF [S1]. It builds on [Voxel-based Representation](10852-voxel-based-representation.md) by using a structured set of voxelized NeRF networks rather than a single global field.
